        "id": 1,
        "options": {
          "content": "## Peak Endurance Model\n\n| Metric | What it measures | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Critical Speed (CS)** | Fastest sustainable velocity \u2014 the aerobic steady-state ceiling | The single best predictor of endurance race performance. Higher CS = faster you can run without accumulating fatigue |\n| **D\u2032 (D-prime)** | Finite work capacity above CS | How many surge metres you have available above threshold \u2014 crucial for climbs and race finishes |\n| **Efficiency Factor (EF)** | Speed per heartbeat (m/s \u00f7 avgHR) | Rising EF = getting faster for the same cardiac cost = improving aerobic economy |\n| **CS:VO2max ratio** | Fraction of aerobic ceiling you can sustain | Differentiates athletes with equal VO2max. Higher ratio = better endurance adaptation |\n| **Cardiac Cost (hrTSS/km)** | Stress per kilometre | Lower = more efficient. Improves with base training and economy work |\n\n_VO2max is your engine size. CS and EF determine how efficiently you drive it._",
          "mode": "markdown"
        },
        "type": "text"
      },
